MKTIME(3)                      P32W Unix                       MKTIME(3)

NAME
       mktime, timegm - convert a date to a time

SYNOPSIS
       #include <time.h>

       time_t mktime(struct tm *tm);
       time_t timegm(struct tm *tm);

DESCRIPTION
       mktime is the inverse of localtime(3): it takes the local date
       and time in tm_year, tm_mon, tm_mday, tm_hour, tm_min and
       tm_sec and returns it as seconds since 1970 (UTC), following
       TZ (tzset(3)). tm_wday, tm_yday and tm_isdst are not read.
       Then it rewrites *tm as localtime gives the result, filling
       in tm_wday, tm_yday and tm_isdst and putting fields that were
       out of range right.

       tm_mday, tm_hour, tm_min and tm_sec may be out of their
       ranges (tm_mday 0 is the last day of the month before; a
       tm_mday of 40 runs into the next month), so mktime does date
       arithmetic. tm_mon must be 0 to 11.

       Whether a time is in summer time is decided by the rule, not
       by tm_isdst: a time in the hour skipped when the clocks go
       forward is taken as standard time (so 02:30 becomes 03:30
       summer time), and one in the hour repeated when they go back
       as summer time.

       timegm takes tm as a UTC time, the inverse of gmtime(3). It
       does not change *tm.

RETURN VALUE
       The time. There is no error return.

EXAMPLES
       struct tm tm;
       memset(&tm, 0, sizeof tm);
       tm.tm_year = 2026 - 1900;
       tm.tm_mon = 11;                 /* December */
       tm.tm_mday = 25;
       t = mktime(&tm);                /* midnight, local time */
       tm.tm_mday += 100;              /* 100 days later */
       t = mktime(&tm);

SEE ALSO
       time(3), tzset(3), date(1)

BUGS
       A tm_mon outside 0 to 11 gives a wrong answer rather than
       being carried into the year. Results past January 2038 do
       not fit in a time_t.
